---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const repo = 'https://github.com/natanatan/Emergent-Epistemic-Ledger';
---
<Base>
  <p class="kicker">Open epistemic infrastructure</p>
  <h1>A ledger for how knowledge changes, not for what is true.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The Emergent Epistemic Ledger (EEL) records scientific claims as signed, append-only events,
    together with the evidence, objections, replications and revisions that shape them.
    Every node rebuilds the same graph of claims from the same history.
  </p>
  <div class="actions">
    <a class="button primary" href="/paper">Read the white paper</a>
    <a class="button" href={repo}>View the code</a>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Ledger consensus is not truth consensus.</strong> The ledger can prove that a contribution was made,
    by whom, when, and that it has not been altered. It never declares a claim true. That judgement stays
    with the people who test it.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Why a ledger for claims</h2>
  <p>
    Research is stored as documents, but a claim lives in a web of dependencies, evidence, objections,
    failed replications and revisions that is scattered across papers and institutions. Negative results
    go unpublished, disagreements are buried in prose, and the history of how a conclusion was reached is
    hard to recover. EEL makes the claim and its history the basic unit, so that history can be inspected,
    reproduced and contested.
  </p>

  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Claims with a history</h3>
      <p>Each claim has a permanent id and links to what it depends on, what supports it, what challenges it, and what it was revised into.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Branches, not a verdict</h3>
      <p>Competing ontologies branch from a shared ancestry. A minority branch is never deleted and can later win on the evidence.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Bounded trust</h3>
      <p>Validators start with authority in named domains only, act by multiple signatures, and their privileges are meant to fade as earned reputation grows.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Replayable evidence</h3>
      <p>Simulations are deterministic, so independent validators can re-run them and check the result byte for byte.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Where it stands</h2>
  <p>
    EEL is an early, locally runnable prototype written in Rust and released under the Apache-2.0 licence.
    It implements the ledger, the ontology projector, domain-limited validation, research requests and
    deterministic simulation replay. It has no cryptocurrency, tokens or payments. It is for exploring the
    protocol, not for anything that needs security.
  </p>
  <p><a href="/code">See what the code does and how to run it →</a></p>
</Base>
